mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — audience profiles that actually help\n\nAbbreviated profiles across business types, each contrasted with the **useless generic persona**.\nNotice every one leads with jobs-to-be-done and real language, separates buyer vs follower where\nrelevant, and skips demographics that don't change the content. (Illustrative.)\n\n---\n\n## 1. B2B SaaS — payroll tool for small-business owners\n- **Who:** owner-operators of 2–30-person businesses who do payroll themselves.\n- **Buyer vs follower:** same person (the owner).\n- **JTBD:** functional — \"run payroll without messing up taxes\"; emotional — \"stop feeling\n  anxious and out of my depth\"; social — \"look like I have my act together.\"\n- **Pains:** terrified of the IRS; never sure it's right; it eats a whole evening.\n- **Objections:** \"switching sounds painful,\" \"probably not built for someone like me.\"\n- **Language bank:** \"I don't even know where to start,\" \"terrified I'll mess up taxes,\" \"just\n  want it off my plate.\" They say \"the payroll stuff,\" not \"payroll operations.\"\n- *Generic persona (useless):* \"Steve, 41, owns a business, $120k revenue, uses LinkedIn.\" → tells\n  you nothing about what to post.\n\n## 2. Local — neighborhood coffee shop\n- **Who:** remote workers and students within ~3 miles looking for a third place.\n- **JTBD:** functional — \"a reliable spot to work for a few hours\"; emotional — \"feel at home,\n  not like a customer\"; social — \"my regular place.\"\n- **Pains:** sketchy wifi elsewhere; cafes that are weird about laptops; feeling anonymous.\n- **Language bank:** \"good wifi and they don't mind if you stay,\" \"they know my order.\"\n- *Generic persona:* \"Coffee-lover Carla, 28, urban.\" → demographic theater.\n\n## 3. Ecommerce / DTC — sustainable sneakers\n- **Who:** design-conscious 25–40s who'd buy them on looks alone.\n- **Buyer vs follower:** often the same; gift-buyers are a second segment.\n- **JTBD:** functional — \"shoes that look good and go with everything\"; emotional — \"feel good\n  about the purchase without trying hard\"; social — \"be the person with the cool sustainable\n  kicks.\"\n- **Objections:** \"sustainable usually means ugly or pricey,\" \"will they actually be comfortable?\"\n- **Language bank:** \"they actually look good,\" \"didn't want to sacrifice style,\" \"the ocean\n  plastic thing is a bonus.\"\n- *Generic persona:* \"Eco-conscious millennial.\" → too broad to write to.\n\n## 4. Personal brand — indie maker building in public\n- **Who:** early/aspiring indie founders; \"their people\" are builders, not hustle-culture fans.\n- **JTBD:** functional — \"learn what actually works from someone real\"; emotional — \"feel less\n  alone in the grind\"; social — \"be part of the build-in-public crowd.\"\n- **Pains:** drowning in generic advice; impostor feelings; performative founder content.\n- **Language bank:** \"actual numbers, not vanity metrics,\" \"tired of gurus,\" \"show me the boring\n  parts.\"\n- *Generic persona:* \"Tech-savvy entrepreneur, 30.\" → invisible.\n\n## 5. Nonprofit — youth literacy (two audiences)\n- **Segment A — supporters/donors:** JTBD: \"give to something that visibly works\"; pains:\n  \"donations vanish into overhead\"; language: \"I want to see the impact,\" \"where does my money\n  go.\"\n- **Segment B — educators/partners:** JTBD: \"get books into my kids' hands without a budget\n  fight\"; language: \"my students have nothing to read at home.\"\n- *Generic persona:* \"Caring donor, 45+.\" → ignores that two very different audiences need\n  different content.\n\n---\n\n## The pattern\n\n- Lead with **jobs-to-be-done** (functional + emotional + social), not demographics.\n- Capture the **real language** — the verbatim phrases — for copy to mirror.\n- Separate **buyer vs follower** and **multiple audiences** where they differ.\n- Include a trait only if it **changes the content**.\n- The test: could a writer who never met them picture a real person and write something that\n  makes that person feel seen? If yes, the profile works.\n"
